<item>
  <title>Google releases emergency security update for eighth zero day flaw CVE-2026-125350 exploited in the wild</title>
  <link>https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/10/google-releases-emergency-update-eighth-chrome-zero-day-cve-2026-125350</link>
  <pubDate>Tue, 10 November 2026 18:30:00 +0300</pubDate>
  <guid isPermaLink="true">https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/10/google-releases-emergency-update-eighth-chrome-zero-day-cve-2026-125350</guid>
  <cd:severity>CRITICAL</cd:severity>
  <cd:cve>CVE-2026-125350</cd:cve>
  <cd:mentions>@[Google](urn:li:organization:1441)‚ @[CISA](urn:li:organization:13010360)</cd:mentions>
  <description><![CDATA[
Browser engine maintainers have distributed out-of-band security updates resolving an actively exploited memory safety vulnerability across desktop platforms. Unauthenticated remote adversaries transmit crafted HTML or XML content to trigger heap corruption and execute arbitrary system commands under user privileges.

The emergency security patch resolves Google Chrome vulnerability CVE-2026-125350, marking the eighth zero day flaw exploited in wild attacks this year. The defect involves a use-after-free vulnerability within internal XML parsing and rendering subroutines. Unauthenticated remote threat actors host malicious web pages or craft targeted phishing links that trigger use-after-free memory corruption during document parsing, escaping browser sandbox protections to achieve arbitrary remote code execution on target host endpoints. Vendor bulletins urge immediate deployment of Chrome desktop release builds 157.0.8103.50 for Linux and 157.0.8103.50/.51 for Windows and Mac platforms.

Subverting core web browser rendering engines introduces immediate endpoint compromise risks across enterprise environments. Because enterprise personnel utilize web browsers for daily SaaS access and web application workflows, unauthenticated browser zero-day exploitation permits threat actors to execute stealth malware payloads, steal active session tokens, and compromise corporate endpoints.

- Deploy Google Chrome emergency security update 157.0.8103.50/.51 across all managed enterprise endpoints via Mobile Device Management MDM immediately.
- Enforce automated application patching and browser update restart rules across corporate workstation fleets.
- Inspect endpoint detection and response EDR telemetry for anomalous process execution trees originating from browser worker processes.
- Restrict untrusted web content execution using browser isolation proxies and zero trust secure web gateways.

Enterprise browser security demands immediate out-of-band patch deployment and strict endpoint process isolation to ensure corporate workstation fleets remain protected against zero-day memory corruption exploits. #CodeDefence #Google #Chrome #ZeroDay #UseAfterFree #RCE #BrowserSecurity #EndpointSecurity #AppSec #PatchManagement
  ]]></description>
</item>

<item>
  <title>Hackers actively exploit critical Barracuda ESG vulnerabilities CVE-2026-116779 to obtain root access</title>
  <link>https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/10/hackers-actively-exploit-critical-barracuda-esg-flaws-cve-2026-116779-root-access</link>
  <pubDate>Tue, 10 November 2026 18:30:00 +0300</pubDate>
  <guid isPermaLink="true">https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/10/hackers-actively-exploit-critical-barracuda-esg-flaws-cve-2026-116779-root-access</guid>
  <cd:severity>CRITICAL</cd:severity>
  <cd:cve>CVE-2026-116779</cd:cve>
  <cd:mentions>@[Barracuda](urn:li:organization:15161044)‚ @[CISA](urn:li:organization:13010360)</cd:mentions>
  <description><![CDATA[
Email gateway security advisories published today warn of active wild attacks targeting perimeter email inspection hardware. Remote unauthenticated threat actors exploit input validation errors and command injection vulnerabilities to achieve unauthenticated root execution on security appliances.

The security advisory covers Barracuda Email Security Gateway ESG vulnerabilities, led by code injection flaw CVE-2026-116779 recently added to the CISA Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog. Remote adversaries transmit malformed email attachments containing crafted commands to exploit unauthenticated input parsing subroutines. Threat telemetry confirms threat actors actively weaponize the vulnerabilities to execute arbitrary system commands under root privileges, drop persistent backdoor daemons, and establish covert access to corporate mail streams. Vendor bulletins mandate immediate software hotfix updates across all appliance models.

Subverting perimeter email gateways undermines organizational communication security and network boundary defense. Because Barracuda ESG appliances process incoming corporate email streams, inspect attachments, and enforce security policies, an unauthenticated root compromise allows threat actors to intercept sensitive email records, alter security rules, and pivot into internal subnets.

- Verify immediate installation of official security hotfixes distributed by Barracuda across all Email Security Gateway appliances.
- Inspect gateway file systems and appliance storage paths for unauthorized script modifications or unverified administrative daemons.
- Restrict public internet access to administrative management web portals, placing management interfaces on isolated out-of-band VLANs.
- Audit corporate email routing logs and credential stores configured on managed email gateway appliances.

Perimeter gateway defense requires emergency security patch application and strict management interface microsegmentation to ensure email security appliances remain protected against unauthenticated command injection exploits. #CodeDefence #Barracuda #ESG #CommandInjection #RCE #RootAccess #CISA #KEV #PerimeterSecurity #NetworkSecurity #AppSec #PatchManagement
  ]]></description>
</item>

<item>
  <title>Turla hackers deploy new Cereal malware toolset to spy on government Microsoft Exchange servers</title>
  <link>https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/10/turla-hackers-deploy-new-cereal-malware-to-spy-on-exchange-servers</link>
  <pubDate>Tue, 10 November 2026 18:30:00 +0300</pubDate>
  <guid isPermaLink="true">https://codedefence.in/blog/2026/11/10/turla-hackers-deploy-new-cereal-malware-to-spy-on-exchange-servers</guid>
  <cd:severity>HIGH</cd:severity>
  <cd:mentions>@[Microsoft](urn:li:organization:1035)</cd:mentions>
  <description><![CDATA[
Cyber threat intelligence reports published today detail sophisticated state-sponsored espionage operations targeting government email infrastructure. Threat actors deployed a novel post-compromise malware framework designed to covertly intercept and exfiltrate sensitive mail store items.

The nation-state campaign, attributed to Russian state-backed threat group Turla, targets Microsoft Exchange server installations across European government entities. Threat actors deploy Cereal, a newly identified malicious toolset operating as an IIS web server module and transport agent. Once installed on compromised Exchange infrastructure, Cereal intercepts incoming and outgoing email messages, logs administrative session tokens, and exfiltrates sensitive diplomatic correspondence without alerting security operations monitoring.

Subverting central enterprise mail servers destroyed data confidentiality across government and corporate communication networks. Because Microsoft Exchange handles core organizational email, scheduling, and contact repositories, stealth transport agent implants permit adversaries to maintain long-term covert surveillance and steal sensitive records undetected.

- Audit Microsoft Exchange server transport agent configurations and IIS web modules for unverified third-party binaries.
- Enforce strict code signing verification across all custom software modules installed on Exchange servers.
- Inspect Exchange server system execution logs for anomalous process spawns or unverified external IP connection attempts.
- Enforce multi-factor authentication MFA and strict zero-trust access controls across all Microsoft Exchange administrative management interfaces.

Enterprise messaging defense demands rigorous module integrity auditing and continuous behavioral monitoring to ensure corporate mail servers remain protected against covert nation-state espionage implants. #CodeDefence #Turla #APT #Microsoft #Exchange #Cereal #Malware #Espionage #EmailSecurity #AppSec #EndpointSecurity
  ]]></description>
